Privacy Policy for the FreeNetRadio Android Application

FreeNetRadio ("the App") is a free, open-source internet radio player. It has no user accounts, no advertising,
no analytics, and no crash reporting. The project runs no servers of its own, so no information about you or your
listening reaches the project or its maintainers.

This policy describes what the App keeps on your device and which third parties your device contacts when you
use it.


Information We Collect

None. There is no backend service behind this App and nothing is transmitted to the project.


Information Stored On Your Device

The App stores the following locally and does not upload any of it:

Your favourite stations, the stations you add yourself, the station played most recently, and your settings.
Station artwork and recent replies from the station directories, kept as a cache and discarded after 24 hours.
The country you selected, or the one determined by the location feature described below.
An image you choose for one of your own stations, if you select one. It is read once, when you pick it, scaled,
and stored inside the App.

Clearing the App's data or uninstalling the App removes all of it.


Location

The App can determine your country to offer you nearby stations. This is optional, happens only when you ask for
it, and requires the coarse location permission, which you can decline or revoke at any time.

When you use it, the App asks Android for a single approximate position and passes it to Android's own geocoder
to obtain a country code. Only that country code is kept; the App neither stores your coordinates nor sends them
to the project or the station directories. Note that the geocoder belongs to your device, not to this App, and on
many devices it is supplied by the device vendor and may resolve the position over the network.


Third-Party Services Your Device Contacts

Browsing and playing stations makes requests directly from your device to:

Community Radio Browser (radio-browser.info), preceded by a DNS lookup used to find one of its API mirrors. These
requests carry the category, country, station list, or search text you asked for.
WebRadioDB (jcorporation.github.io), for its station datasets and station images.
The stream server of the station you play, and any server that station redirects to.

Like any network request, these reveal your IP address, the time of the request, and what you asked for to the
service receiving it. Requests carry a User-Agent that by default names the App, its version, and your Android
version; you can change it in the settings. These services are operated by others and have their own privacy
practices, which this policy does not cover.

Many internet radio streams are offered only over unencrypted HTTP, and the App allows such streams. Their
contents, including the station you are listening to, can be observed on the network path.


Diagnostic Reports

The App never sends logs or crash data on its own, on a schedule, or in the background.

If you choose "Prepare diagnostic report" in the settings, the App collects its own log output together with
information about your device and build, such as manufacturer, model, Android version, and build fingerprint. The
report is then handed to Android's share chooser so that you can pick an application, review the message, choose
the recipient, and decide whether to send it. Transmission is entirely up to you and the application you choose.
There is no "always send" option; each report requires a fresh decision.


Other Applications On Your Device

Station artwork is served through a content provider that other applications on your device can read. It holds
station images downloaded from the public station directories, and any image you chose for a station of your own.


Children's Privacy

The App is not directed at children and collects nothing from anyone, including children.


Changes To This Policy

Any change to this policy will appear in this file, in the App's public source repository.


